    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92312.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 8,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, inshAllah. So let's get into it. I think it's fairly uncontroversial to say that you're a transformational figure in the social and political discourse, similar to Obama. Your existence and the matter that you've chosen to live your life in a public way as a politician, I think, proves that. The next president wrote the forward to your book. You're famous. I want doing my prep for this and seeing all of the new podcasts that you've been on and shows you've been on. It's a groundbreaking win, and I understand certainly that sentiment. But the question that keeps popping into my mind is whether you will be a transformational politician. In my view, Obama wasn't. In my view, Obama was a failure. Obama believed that he was part of an apparatus that he couldn't really impact. And I read the quote from the book to start the podcast simply because it's indicative of that, of being in a position that not much can be done. There is a big There's a big concern about perception and politics so that the criticism that might happen could potentially harm negotiations down the road and obama finishes that section basically saying you know we didn't want to torpedo our chances of passing a future reform bill uh... so i i'm glad they did that would you like to i mean he could uh... did he get into the bill that the the the the immigration reform that they passed a sweeping you know immigration changes that got passed um... away he didn't So, you know, again, this is my concern and I wonder whether you've read the book and I wonder what your takeaway is in that, you know, about that and about sort of being in an apparatus where yes, you are in the room and you are at the table. But what is happening at that table and what are the things that you're thinking about in trying to actually sort of accomplish some sort of progress in the legislature?",
