    "turn_text": "just the first term is my understanding. But it's interesting because you see Obama really grapple with With his own beliefs in the possibility for progress within the political sphere, with the compromises that he made, you know, on a micro level you see him sort of defend each of those decisions, but then on a macro level I think the broader theme is questioning whether they're worth it. And I haven't gotten through the entire book, so I'll reserve judgment. I mean, I think on the specific point, I'll reserve judgment in terms of where he comes out on it. But on the specific point that you raised around the immigration reform package, I think in retrospect, most folks who want immigration reform have come to the conclusion that that was the wrong decision because it was precisely the kind of strategic decision that betrayed a more fundamental first principle. That inevitably, strategic and tactical decisions will have to be made when it comes to advancing legislation. But the sort of question is, in terms of who we want at the table, are they people who will abide by certain first principles when making those decisions? Or will they let the short-term political imperatives or the politics of possibility serve as an excuse to undermine the overall moral questions involved? And I think in the instance of immigration reform, President Obama did that. He let a short-term political strategic consideration become an excuse to violate the human dignity and rights of many undocumented people who faced deportation during his administration. So I think it's a good example of how Everyone will be required to make strategic and tactical decisions and advancing legislation. The question is, when you're faced with those decisions do abide by certain first principles. You know, for me, going into this role. My hope is that at the end of my term or at the end of my time in the General Assembly, for however long the voters will have me, that the most notable component of my service will not be my gender identity, that it will be the legislation that we pass. And I think there are always going to be challenges. And I think you and I have talked about this, When you go into spaces that are inherently flawed because of the structures of government we have, because of the society and systems that we have, because of money and politics, it's easy to make mistakes. And I can assure you, I will make mistakes. My goal is to be able to come back to my constituents, to this district, to advocates and explain my rationale. Even if they disagree, hopefully they can understand it. But hopefully more often than not, they will agree with the decisions that are made. I think right now we're at a significant moment. I think. In many ways, the ability of someone to help bring about significant change is not just a byproduct of who they are, what their principles are, and what role they're in. It's also a byproduct of the circumstances. And I think the incoming general assembly is coming into office or returning to office at a time of really significant challenges. where a number of issues have come to the fore that offer significant opportunities for progress. And it's on us to seize that moment, to meet that moment, and to make that progress, whether we're talking about obviously the current public health crisis, whether we're talking about the preexisting need for Delaware to figure out our sort of next economic chapter. whether we're talking about the once in a generation call for racial justice, whether we're talking about the lawsuit in the education equity or the settlement in the education equity lawsuit. Those are four major challenges that in a traditional year could fill an entire legislative calendar. We are going to be called because of circumstances to address all four of them. And I think that that moment and the lessons that the public has I think learned from the last year in particular provide us with a unique responsibility and a unique opportunity to hopefully meet this moment with significant progress. And I'm happy to get into my thoughts on all of those specific areas. But I really think it's a significant moment for us.",
