  {
    "episode_id": 92312,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92312.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 12,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, I do too. And I mention your position as a transformative social person and public person only because I think that is one of the things that gives you a particular bit of political power walking into a freshman legislature that maybe other people may not have because of maybe the connections you've had, the book you've written, and some other positions that you've taken in your public life. But my concern is really when you take all of those issues and put them together is one of perspective. And I'll tell you what I mean. Are you familiar with the term capitalist realism? It's a term that Mark Mark Fisher created the term and it basically it at the core of it It basically says that whatever our problems are they have to be solved in in a in a preset way that we've all well the idea is that we've all agreed that it can't be any other way I'll give you an example one of the first public Confrontations that I had with Governor Carney I asked him a question about increasing the LLC fees or making the tax code more progressive that's come up in the legislature many times before. His response was one of capital flight. All these people will leave the only way that we can basically expand the tax base is by incenting corporations on the supply side is by keeping taxes incredibly low, by keeping spending as low as we possibly can. And this is the only way that's going to do this. Now, I actually think he really believes that. I think he's absolutely wrong. It's a failure of imagination and it's a failure of really deep sort of political thought. But he lives in that world where everyone's supposed to accept as sort of as canon, as gospel, that that is the confines in which these problems need to be addressed can only be done like this. And I don't agree with that. And I'm wondering what your thoughts about it are.",
