  {
    "episode_id": 92312,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92312.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 13,
    "turn_text": "Well, I think one of the things I've learned in my advocacy is that you really do have to meet people where they are. And we might all have different perspectives with which we see the particular issues we face. So for someone, it might be a moral perspective. For others, it might be an economic perspective. And I think one of the things I try to do is I try to meet people where they are and articulate the goals that I have in a way that resonates for them with their experience and with their perspective and with the language that they use. For some people that also might be the language of faith, right? There are various sort of perspectives and languages we see the world through. And I think making sure we meet people where they are. And so to your point around this question of of both economic and financial stability or long-term stability, I think there's an argument for progressive policies through that lens, that one, we have a need to diversify our state revenue beyond the overall alliance on the franchise tax, that that has grown significantly since the 80s, that it is It has actually resulted in us not having a significant of a deficit this year as we otherwise would during this COVID pandemic. But in the long term, we need to diversify our revenue and remove our sort of over-reliance on that tax. The only way to do that is to attract more young families to Delaware. The only way to do that is to expand the income tax base to build the kind of workforce that businesses are looking for. Because at the end of the day, Alabama, Mississippi, South Dakota, they might have low tax rates, but we don't necessarily see them attracting the kind of businesses and being an incubator for businesses like we see in places like California and Washington and Oregon and Massachusetts. or New York for that matter, that the only way that we build that economy, that next chapter in our economy is to attract the kind of workforce we need, the only way to address our over-reliance on the franchise tax, which I think frankly most political leaders in Delaware agree has gotten out of hand, is to attract more young families to Delaware. And everyone I think agrees with that.",
