    "turn_idx": 22,
    "turn_text": "Yeah. The short answer is yes. Nothing that you said there, I oppose. Everything you said there, I support. I support moving away from a corporate subsidy policy for economic growth. I support making them more progressive state income tax. I support Medicaid buy-in. I support, you know, I ran my campaign on universal paid family and medical leave, which would be the most significant social safety net expansion in Delaware, probably since the seed scholarship was created, if you would consider the seed scholarship a social safety net program. I support a $15 minimum wage. I haven't had conversations. My sense is that the progressive cost. My sense is that it's mostly in that it's in the house. I haven't had. I'd want to hear more about the strategic and tactical decisions that the caucus is making. But I do believe that we were elected to do big things. I believe we have a unique moment to do big things. If they're frankly, I've already said, if there are votes that I need to take that make my job harder and getting reelected, I am happy to take those votes. And there are absolutely things that I would be very, very, very willing to say, I don't care if these are popular or unpopular, they're the right things to do. There's plenty of those. So in terms of a specific caucus, I'm open, I haven't had any overtures about that. But I think what you will, Certainly here, if you talk to the members that you mentioned, similarly, progressives in the Senate, they will tell you that I am as staunchly progressive and bold in my desire for an agenda and a vision for this coming legislative session as any other member.",
