    "turn_text": "You know, I can't predict the future either, but I think there's never been the momentum and possibility like we have right now. And I think that the new members of the General Assembly have really, I think, reinforced and empowered and motivated a lot of longer-term members that maybe have felt like we weren't seeing the kind of significant progress that they wanted to see. And so I think that between the new members and a lot of the returning members, I don't think we've ever had a moment of such frankly, limitless possibility before us, whether that pans out, obviously is going to be those on all of us who are there now. And I think you're right. I think underlying all of your questions is a point that I am very aware of. And I have had conversations with many in advocacy and in the progressive movement where they say that I have a unique position platform and opportunity to leverage whatever I bring to the table for significant progress. And, you know, I've had conversations with people, particularly during the campaign where I was like, that may be true. I think there are questions of how people respond to me when I get there, but I do Since starting the job two months ago, do see that in certain instances. And it is certainly my intention to show folks that I'm willing to utilize that position I'm in to be an ally to the progressive and movement and the fight for significant progress here in Delaware. I think that you know, there is, there's, there's, you know, you mentioned my constituency. First off, this district looks like Delaware. Demographically, the economic diversity of Delaware with the exception of, you know, not an agricultural industry here. We effectively represent that both economic and demographic diversity of the state. And so we've got, yes, the Highlands, but we've also got, you know, the Ninth Ward, we've got Bell thought we've got claim on. We've got significant diversity and all of those communities are my base because all of those communities are my district. And to the degree that there's specific language that I can use here in the Highlands to make clear to folks who are doing well that the reality is actually also that the long term that the status quo will not work for any of us long term. And that the only way to write that next economic chapter is for us to get creative, to be innovative and to pass these best practice policies. I mean, these are best practice, the progressive policies are best practices. And we are seeing them pay dividends in other states when it comes to economic growth and shared prosperity from that economic growth.",
