  {
    "episode_id": 92312,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92312.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 25,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, I mean, we had a conversation a month or two ago, and I said it here. The solve to my cynicism is action. The opportunity is there. It's just a matter of seizing it and making a real effort to build a coalition to do some of it. I think what'll help, and I don't know if you have any insight to this, I know I've talked to a lot of members recently about the setup of the General Assembly, and it'll be all virtual at least for the first couple of months, and I think that's necessary. It's unfortunate, but there's no other, there's no way around it. But I do think that having the building open or doing things in public in some fashion could also help with this. I go back to thinking about Obama after reading the book and realizing that You know, he very sort of disingenuously used to say, you know, I can't do it myself. You have to make me do it. You have to bring pressure to bear to make me do it. But he completely ignored the fact that he let the grassroots machine that got him elected die on the vine. There's a very good essay about this in the New Republic. what's called, Obama's Lost Army, Micah Sifrai wrote it. So I encourage people to take a look at that. And it actually will tell you that he was probably right, and that's probably why he let the grassroots machine sort of die, so that the status quo was easier to maintain. But I hope that there is gonna be some opportunity to sort of publicly have our voices heard, Do you know how that's going to work in this session? Is there any idea? Is there a target where either leg hall can be open or other things can be done in public in some fashion? Or are we just sort of playing like wait and see at this point?",
