  {
    "episode_id": 92312,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92312.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 26,
    "turn_text": "I think my sense and obviously I'm not part of leadership so I'm not part of the day-to-day conversations but certainly my sense is that for January the decision is made that we're virtual and that it will be re-evaluated sort of on a rolling basis depending on the scale of the spread, the progress of the vaccine, frankly, the new, more contagious strain presents another X factor that's hard to take into account when planning long-term. So my sense is there will be a reevaluation. I'm under no illusions that doing this virtual is harder. It's harder from the perspective of much of the actual sort of legislating the conversations, you know, the ability to hash out an issue is a lot easier in person than it is on the phone or a resume. And that the young legislators, the new legislators, not the young, the new legislators are at a disadvantage because, you know, You learn so much by osmosis being there that it's hard. It's hard to start the legislative process with the start of it being completely remote for you. So there's significant challenges that we face. I think from a public input perspective, I think they're trade-offs. The virtual session allows for the public to participate in a certainly one here in watch and therefore hold people accountable for what they're doing and saying more than it has been in the past where the sessions were not live streamed and where being able to watch the Senate session required you to take time off work, have the means to get down there, have the ability to get down there, et cetera. So that there are elements that are more transparent and more accessible than things have been in the past. But then, of course, it's much more difficult to organize a lot of people to put pressure on legislators by being physically in legislative hall. And as someone who worked on an advocacy effort, I know how important physically being there was. So I think there's trade-offs. I do think it's harder virtually and my hope my very strong hope is that the virus and the pandemic is in a place where we can we can go back at some point this year. Because I do think there is a unique window and it is harder now. I haven't done the job yet virtually. So my ability to say how hard that is will be, you know, informed over the next few weeks as we actually start the",
