  {
    "episode_id": 92312,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92312.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 30,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, well, I think you summed up the issues really well there. From my perspective, I think that this moment calls for a meaningful economic agenda. I think Delaware has done a really good job making progress on issues that tend to not cost much. But I think we have to be willing to invest, recognizing that it's both the right thing to do, and it's actually smart long-term when it comes to the financial questions. And so I think that that means an economic agenda that truly rebuilds Delaware from this pandemic in a way that lifts up working families. I think at the heart of that is obviously a $15 minimum wage. At the heart of that is paid family and medical leave. I mean, I think there are few issues more sort of more at the heart of the confluence of the economic crisis and the public health crisis than the need for medical paid medical leave. And so I think paid family medical leave needs to be a part of that. And I think that you mentioned, I think beyond those two big economic issues, I also think that, as I mentioned before, the educational equity lawsuit and the settlement that comes from that, but then more broadly, the actual specifics of those policies. This is, we have not updated our education funding structure in more than 80 years. I think we have the oldest education funding structure in the country. And there are some things we do that only a small handful of states do and there's some things we do that literally no other state does. And so I think that along with that educational or that economic package, we have to have meaningful education, a meaningful education package that really addresses the structural inequities in our school funding system. I think those three. I think the criminal justice package is necessary. Obviously, the task force is putting that together. I think one thing that I'm very eager to see in that is a real reform of the law enforcement bill of rights. especially the component that seals the records of law enforcement officers, even if they have been fired from their job for excessive force or dishonesty, the ceiling of those records for other jurisdictions when hiring law enforcement, the challenges that defense attorneys face when trying to obtain those records in a criminal case, and the lack of public access to those records. completely cut off any more meaningful reforms and accountabilities that we can put in place. So I think at minimum we've got to see a starting place of a real reform of Leo Boer. So I think those four issues I think are must-haves. But then I think when we're talking about what does our economy look like in the future, And how do we reform our criminal justice system? When we talk about the confluence of those issues, obviously you see marijuana legalization there. My hope is that we pass it in a way too that addresses the records of folks who've been incarcerated for any marijuana related offense, as well as making sure that the new market is keeping capital that exists in communities that have been criminalized by the criminalization first of use and now of the market, that we keep capital in those communities in a new legalized market. And so I'm hopeful that we'll see progress on that. And on top of that, I think we're also gonna see progress that's left over from last year around building a green future for our state. That includes increasing the renewable portfolio standard. But I also think it includes hopefully a more fundamental look at things like electric vehicles and making them more economical and accessible for people. Making sure that solar is more accessible and economical for people. actually investing in and creating a state commitment to wind energy here in Delaware. My understanding is where you're the only state on the East Coast that doesn't have a meaningful investment and commitment to wind energy. So I think there's, like I said, there's a lot. There's a lot. And in prioritization, I think the reality is that the circumstances call for progress in some form or fashion on all of those issues because the question has been called on each of them.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Assert / Elaborate"
  },
