  {
    "episode_id": 92312,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92312.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 42,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, I think that, you know, whenever someone frustratingly complaints to me about voters who don't vote. You know, my point is that's an indictment of That's an indictment of our politics. It's not an indictment of those voters. It means that we have not given them the reason and the motivation to come out because we haven't delivered enough for them. And so I feel a strong responsibility, not just to a movement, but to the voters across this district and across Delaware to say, We know times are tough, and we know that the crisis that we're in has only reinforced and laid bare the inequities in our system that predated it. And we know that you have elected us in the manner that you've elected us, right, at a record number, or not a record number, but at a higher number. And with a lot of new people who understand the urgency of these issues, that the message is clear. And that we will take these two years to do as much as we can. Now, to your point, we're not going to win every single battle. We're not going to have a major advancement on every single issue. But I do think that we have a generational opportunity before us to do quite a bit and to have folks when they go out and vote in 2022 know that the last two years have really, have, have, have resulted in really meaningful change. And that includes meaningful change when it comes to the economic conditions and the financial stability and security that families here in the state face.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
